course: dictionary
lesson: 5411
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The words around it, its context, show you which meaning the writer means. «Labour» is a good example: it can mean hard work, the workers in a company, or the work of giving birth.
explain: «The farmer's labour in the fields lasted all summer.» Here labour means hard work done with the body. Now read: «The company paid its labour well.» Here labour means the workers, and the work they do for pay. Check by swapping your meaning into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
explain: Labour with a capital L is a name, not a job. «In the UK the two biggest parties for a long time have been Labour and the Conservatives.» Labour is the name of a political party there, and the capital letter is a clue too.
fact: Labour means hard work, especially work you do with your body.
fact: Labour can also mean the workers in a company, or the work they do for pay.
fact: To labour means to work hard or to move with great effort.
fact: In everyday life, «go into labour» means start giving birth to a baby.
fact: Child labour means children doing hard jobs for little pay, as in history lessons.
exercise: «In the UK the two biggest parties for a long time have been Labour and the Conservatives.» In this sentence, Labour means | *A political party | Hard work done with the body | Giving birth to a baby
exercise: Mia says, «Mowing the whole lawn was real labour!» What does labour mean here? | A baby being born | A political party | The workers in a company | *Hard work done with the body
exercise: Ben says, «She has gone into labour, so we must call the nurse.» What does labour mean here? | A famous political party | Workers paid for their time | Hard work with a spade | *Giving birth to a baby
test: «The company paid its labour well, so the workers were happy.» What does labour mean here? | A political party | Giving birth | *The workers, and the work they do for pay | Hard work done with the body
test: Sam said, «I laboured up the hill with my heavy bag.» What does laboured mean? | *Moved with great effort | Sang happily | Sat down to rest | Ran very fast
exam: Jamie reads, «Child labour was common long ago.» Which best explains child labour? | Children who are very tall | Children playing in a park | Children who vote for a political party | *Children doing hard jobs for little pay
